Done. The round's work is in `impl/gen_batchlane.c` and a full `Round gen_r4` section in `strategies/gen_batchlane.md`.

**What happened this round:** I discovered the A/B protocol everyone has been using is confounded — `tryout.sh` leases a fresh slot per invocation, so "control-first adjacent pairs" often run on *different cores* in different turbo states (10–25% apart while MKL moves <2%). Re-racing everything with same-core interleaved best-of-N flipped one shipped default: the sched-pressure attribute at L=15 actually **loses** 4.3% (r1–r3 had it backwards); it honestly wins at 10/12 and stays off at 20. I also built and rejected a per-column map epilogue (spill-free pencil + L1-hot map loop — the cross-invocation protocol said −4.5%/−8.7% at 15/20, the honest protocol says it loses everywhere; kept as `-DBL_EPI*` cross-arch knobs), and found gcc 11 ignores scalar constants as an embedded-broadcast pressure cure (byte-identical binary). Chain outputs stay bit-identical to r3; all gates pass at all four sizes including B=1 and mixed-remainder batches.

**One-liner:** Twiddle-free SoA-8 batch-lane PFA with register-explicit pencils and eager fused rsqrt14/rcp14 map, sched-pressure now on 10/12 only after a same-core interleaved re-race — batched chain 1.157 / 1.919 / 4.567 / 13.394 µs/xform at L=10/12/15/20 (4.0–4.5× MKL, L=15 −4.3% vs r3), B=1 10.5 / 17.4 / 41.7 / 116.5 µs; single-call rel L2 = 2.6–3.1e-16, two-step gate ≤1.2e-15, chains 1.1–1.6× honest anchors.
